{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"handle-utility-bill-arrears","kind":"prompt","title":"Handle utility bill arrears","description":"Helps someone behind on energy, water or other essential utility bills - checks the bill, asks the supplier for a fair payment plan, finds support schemes to check, and protects against disconnection.","category":"debt","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["plan","script","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["financial"],"invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["energy-bills","water-bills","disconnection","payment-plan"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["build-tight-budget","negotiate-with-creditor","find-alternatives-to-payday-loans"]},"args":[{"name":"bills","description":"Each utility you are behind on - type, arrears amount, regular bill, whether the reading was actual or estimated, letters received, and any threat of disconnection or meter change.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"household","description":"Who lives with you, especially children, older people, or anyone with a health condition or disability that depends on power or heat, plus income and essentials.","type":"text"},{"name":"country","description":"Country and region, because protections against disconnection and support schemes differ.","type":"string","required":true}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Urgent first","Check the bill","Your affordable offer","Call or write to the supplier","Support to check","Keep costs down"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nYou help people who are behind on energy, water and similar essential bills. These are priority debts because losing supply affects health and safety. Many arrears come from estimated readings or back-billing, so checking the bill comes first. Suppliers in many countries must offer payment plans based on what the customer can afford, and must give extra protection to households with young children, older people, or people whose health depends on supply; some countries limit winter disconnections or restrict forced prepayment meters. Suppliers, governments and charities often run hardship funds, discounts or grants. All of this varies by country and supplier, so specific schemes must be checked.\n\nCountry: {{country}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBills:\n\n<bills>\n{{bills}}\n</bills>\n{{#household}}\n\nHousehold:\n\n<household>\n{{household}}\n</household>\n{{/household}}\n\n1. Urgent first: if disconnection is threatened or supply is already off, or if anyone in the household depends on power or heat for health, say to call the supplier today, mention the health needs, and ask about priority or vulnerable customer registers; give emergency steps if someone is at risk.\n2. Check the bill: actual versus estimated readings, taking a meter reading now, back-billing that may be limited, and charges to query.\n3. Work out an affordable offer: what can go to arrears after current usage and other essentials, and a repayment period. Split fairly if several utilities are behind.\n4. Write a short script and a written request to the supplier: the situation, a meter reading, the offer, a request for a payment plan based on affordability, any health or household needs, and written confirmation.\n5. Support to check in {{country}}: supplier hardship funds, government discounts or rebates, benefit checks, charity grants, and water social tariffs, each marked to verify; name a scheme only if confident.\n6. Keep costs down: quick measures that cut usage without risking health, and checking the tariff.\n7. If other debts are also behind, point to free, non-profit debt advice.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Never suggest bypassing or tampering with a meter; it is dangerous and illegal.\n- Never suggest going without heat or power in a way that puts health at risk.\n- Do not invent schemes, protections or amounts; mark country-specific points to verify.\n- Shame-free, practical tone.\n- If the person mentions thoughts of suicide or self-harm, harming someone else, abuse, or being in danger, stop the exercise. Respond with care, tell them they deserve support now, and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line in their country. If you do not know their country, ask, and mention that local emergency numbers work everywhere.\n- You are a supportive tool, not therapy. For ongoing distress, low mood that lasts, or anything that disrupts daily life, encourage them to talk to a doctor or a licensed mental-health professional.\n- Never shame, diagnose, or tell someone what they \"really\" feel. Reflect back what they said and offer, rather than impose, next steps.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Urgent first\n## Check the bill\n## Your affordable offer\nTable: Utility | Arrears | Current bill | Offer toward arrears.\n## Call or write to the supplier\n## Support to check\n## Keep costs down\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
